   74 ## 2. Estimand
   75 
   76 For rule r in {R3 (F111), C*, B3 (F000)}, fresh market m and churn rate p:
   77 
   78 g_p(r, m) = mean over the 200 evaluation seeds of the normalised gain of the D2+H3 attack selected by the registered
   79 search under churn p, against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn.
   80 
   81 Contrasts: d_p(m) = g_p(C*, m) - g_p(R3, m) and q_p(m) = g_p(B3, m) - g_p(R3, m), pooled with equal market weights, and
   82 their changes from the registered no-churn values on the same seeds. The attack and reference profits are reported
   83 separately. These are differences between finite searches with a fixed reference candidate in a changed environment, not
   84 identified optimal values. A weaker reference can raise a measured gain, and unequal reference losses can move either
   85 contrast in either direction.
   86 
   87 ## 3. Cells and execution contract
   88 
   89 - **Churn model.** Each of the nine honest opponents plays honest-max delivery and offer and, in each round, retires its
   90   identity and re-enters under a fresh one with probability p (`docs/revision/churn/churn_v2.py` `HonestChurn`). The draw
   91   uses the episode's `policy_seed[t, e]`, so it is deterministic and the same for every rule on the same seed. The model
   92   keeps provider capacity and quality fixed and changes identity age; it also resets an honest provider's history and
   93   lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information alone. **Rates p in {0.01, 0.05}** are illustrative
   94   sensitivity settings without empirical calibration: mean identity lifetimes of 100 and 20 rounds, about 18 and 89
   95   honest replacements per episode (measured, section 1). Whether the focal identity stays the newest is measured (section 4).
   96 - **Cells.** {R3, C*, B3} x fresh0..fresh5 x {0.01, 0.05} at rho 0.2, D2+H3, registered search budget (5 restarts, 300
   97   evaluations, 5 training seeds), the market's 200 evaluation seeds, a doubling check with its held-out evaluation on
   98   every cell: **36 cells**, plus the gate cell.
   99 - **Driver.** `docs/revision/churn/churn_v2.py` (under `docs/`: the code freeze allows changes only there). It runs the
  100   frozen `revision_v2` stage functions through `_run_one` and reuses the reset driver's helpers
  101   (`docs/revision/followup/reset_followup_v2.py`: input copies, pre-episode checks, follow-up freeze, acceptance,
  102   comparators, inventory). `install()` wraps three `revision_v2` names, also in every pool worker's initialiser (tested):
  103   `train_cell` (which `eval_cell` calls), so search, twin search, the doubling stage's reference menu, and evaluation run
  104   under churn; `select_reference`, so a cell with churning opponents never reuses a candidate's stored no-churn training
  105   profits (only the abstain candidate, whose profit is zero, keeps its value); and `reference_of`, so a churn job's
  106   frozen reference carries its training-seed score evaluated under the same churn, which the doubling stage's
  107   `reference_change` subtracts. Churn job ids carry the suffix `|churn<p>` so they never overwrite the copied registered
  108   D2+H3 records that the reference menus read; each rate writes to its own directory (`results/v2/followup-churn/p<p>/`),
  109   never to `eval/all`.
  110 - **References: frozen candidate, played under churn.** Each (market, rule) keeps its frozen truthful reference
  111   candidate. Two reselections are distinguished. Reselecting among the frozen menu's candidates under churn needs only
  112   their evaluation on the training seeds; the doubling stage does it for every cell (with the fix above), and its
  113   `reference_change` is reported as a diagnostic, not as a bound on held-out bias. Regenerating the menus under churn
  114   would need 60 D2+H3 and 60 X7-SC searches per rate, about 12.19 USD per rate before the churn slowdown (section 9); it
  115   is not done.
  116 - **Checks before any episode** (reset driver, plan draft 5): configuration hash equals the container hash; salt; known
  117   commit; runner and `sim/` equal protocol-v2; each job's runner, salt, configuration, root, seeds, rule parameters and
  118   context equal its frozen reference's provenance; output not inside `--src` or `eval/all`. Inputs are copied read-only
  119   and hash-checked against the original records and freeze; the follow-up freeze and acceptance run before evaluation
  120   and doubling; a final inventory records every output hash, the environment, and the churn driver's hash.
  121 - **Validation before spend (free, on the Mac).** (a) Unit tests: the churn policy is truthful and exits at rate p,
  122   deterministically; p = 0 never exits; the wrapper adds churning opponents to training and evaluation cells, also in
  123   pool workers, and leaves other jobs unchanged; churn ids and provenance are distinct; under churn every menu candidate
  124   is rerun on the five training seeds and the frozen reference's score is recomputed; newcomer counts, unavailable
  125   bins, the focal newest share, market pooling; the gate's id mapping; a failed gate stops dispatch; the cap check. (b)
  126   A smoke-size run of the whole path for fresh0 R3 at p = 0.05, including the gate path, both winners' diagnostics, and
  127   the memory preflight on its output; production refuses a missing `--started`, `--gate skip`, and a separate stage
  128   without a saved passing gate; the worker limit fits the memory limit. All pass: `uv run pytest -q docs/revision/churn`
  129   (23 passed: also the bit-identity pod gate, the preflight's real-task sample, and admission after the preflight with
  130   the kept worker limit).
  131 - **(c) The p = 0 gate, first on the pod.** The driver runs fresh0 R3 D2+H3 at p = 0 through the injected path (inputs,
  132   search, freeze, acceptance, evaluation, doubling) into `gate-p0/`, maps the id `|churn0` to the registered id, and
  133   compares search and evaluation by the reset driver's `compare` and the doubling check by `compare_doubling`
  134   (draft-5 criteria of the reset plan). **On the pod the gate requires bit identity** (`--require-identical`: search,
  135   evaluation and doubling identical to the registered records, the reset driver's `pod_gate_ok` semantics, the author's
  136   decision); on the Mac the draft-5 tolerance criterion applies. It saves `gate-p0/gate.json` (with `identical`,
  137   `require_identical` and `gate_ok`) and an inventory. On failure the driver stops before
  138   any churn cell is dispatched and reports. Every later production stage, also when run alone or resumed, first requires
  139   that saved gate record: passing its criterion (on the pod, bit-identical), for this cell and id mapping, and run by the
  140   same driver version (its hash in the gate's inventory); production runs cannot skip the gate.
  141 
  142 ## 4. Diagnostics (evaluation only, no search)
  143 
  144 For every cell, the base and the doubled winner are rerun on the evaluation seeds under churn (`diagnostics` stage):
  145 per-seed normalised profits; per episode the [served, audited] counts by identity age, in bins [0, 10), [10, 20),
  146 [20, 50), [50, 200), for honest replacement identities, honest initial identities, and the focal provider's identities;
  147 honest replacements per episode; and per episode two "newest" shares for the focal identity. (i) Creation-time newest:
  148 the share of rounds in which its creation round is strictly the latest among all current identities; this is not the
  149 order R3 uses. (ii) Mechanism-relevant: over the rounds in which it holds assigned jobs, the share in which its
  150 first-offer round is strictly the latest among the identities holding assigned jobs, which is how R3 ages and ranks
  151 identities (`sim/mechanisms/factorial.py`). Only (ii) is used to discuss R3's targeting.
  152 A bin with no served job is reported as unavailable. Pooling over markets: per rule, winner and bin, the mean of the
  153 market shares over the markets where the share is available, with that count. A higher audited share of honest
  154 newcomers is greater audit exposure; it is not by itself a financial loss.
  155 
  156 ## 5. Analysis (fixed now)
  157 
  158 - Per market and pooled g_p(r), d_p, q_p, with paired seed bootstrap intervals (9,999 resamples, percentile, 95 percent;
  159   the same resampled seed indices for every rule and rate of a market; pooled replicates resample seeds within each
  160   market and average the six market means). The intervals condition on the six markets and the selected policies; they
  161   do not include search uncertainty or uncertainty across markets.
  162 - Changes from no churn, paired by seed with the registered records: base with base, doubled with doubled.
  163 - Attack and reference profit terms separately; the reselection diagnostic per cell; sign counts over the six markets.
  164 - No confirmatory test, no multiplicity family, no change to any registered decision; all results are labelled
  165   exploratory follow-up.
  166 
  167 ## 6. Pre-specified expectations
  168 
  169 Written after the disclosed peek in section 1 and before any churn search. Scored on point estimates, as met (holds
  170 under both the base and the doubled searches, each against its own no-churn baseline), failed (fails under both), or not
  171 established (the two disagree).
  172 
  173 1. Pooled g_0.05(R3) exceeds the registered pooled no-churn g(R3) (base 0.0649; doubled against the doubled value).
  174 2. Pooled d_0.05 = C* - R3 is below the registered no-churn value (base 0.0207; doubled 0.0296).
  175 3. q_0.01 = B3 - R3 is positive on all six markets.
  176 4. At p = 0.05, the market-pooled audited share of honest replacement identities in their first ten rounds is higher
  177    under R3 than under C* and than under B3, for both winners (a cross-rule difference under churn; no no-churn baseline
  178    exists for honest replacement identities in these cells).
  179 
  180 A failure is not read as evidence of a particular cause. Only if expectation 2 is met may the paper say that R3's
  181 measured edge over C* shrinks when honest opponents churn; only if 4 is met that, under churn, R3 exposes honest newcomers
  182 to more audits than C* and B3 do.
  183 
  184 ## 7. Cost and operations
  185 
  186 **Work** (`experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json`, 0.108 USD per core-hour): per cell a D2+H3 search (2,084 s), the
  187 evaluation on 200 seeds (96.0 s per 160 seeds, scaled: 120 s), the doubling check with its held-out evaluation (998 s),
  188 and two diagnostic profiles (60 s); churn slows an episode by 1.133 (p = 0.01) and 1.330 (p = 0.05) (section 1). 36
  189 cells plus the gate cell: **41.1 core-hours, 4.43 USD** at the benchmark price.
  190 
  191 **Memory** (`docs/revision/churn/churn_memory.py`): one 200-seed profile peaks at 981 MB without churn and 1,084 MB at
  192 p = 0.05; an evaluate or doubling task keeps four profiles (2.5 GB without churn, operations ledger), so about 2.8 GB
  193 under churn. Workers: search 16; evaluate, doubling and diagnostics 9 (about 25 GB, 23.3 GiB, below the pod's 28 GiB floor; an
  194 independent four-profile check at p = 0.05 in review peaked at 2,532 MiB). **Pod-side preflight** before the evaluate and
  195 the doubling stage (`memory_preflight`): real tasks in child processes, one evaluate task per rule and one doubling task,
  196 each writing its production record (not run again); the largest peak times a margin of 1.25 for variation across
  197 markets, rules and winners; the cgroup limit and the parent's own peak; workers = min(current limit,
  198 floor((0.9 x limit - parent) / (1.25 x peak))), saved in `memory_preflight_<stage>.json`; the driver refuses if no task
  199 fits. The chosen limit is kept for every later stage, the diagnostics included (two profiles per task, below the
  200 evaluate task). **Admission and the preflight:** before a stage the cost admission covers the preflight's serial tasks
  201 plus the remaining jobs at the current limit; after the preflight it runs again for the remaining jobs at the chosen
  202 limit, and stops between stages if the cap would be passed.
  203 
  204 **Billed pod time** (one 16-vCPU pod, 0.56 USD per hour): the gate cell serially, then per rate the 18 searches in two
  205 waves of 16 and the evaluation, doubling and diagnostics in two waves of 9, plus 0.6 hours of preparation and idle:
  206 **5.95 hours, 3.33 USD**.
  207 
  208 **Budget** (author, 2026-11-10). The arm uses the shared ledger category `followup` (cap 22 USD for all follow-up runs).
  209 Before the pod: `docs/revision/followup/budget_followup.py --reserve churn-pod 4.43` (the unpadded estimate; the
  210 wrapper reserves 4.43 x 1.25 = 5.54 USD). **Stop cap 8.00 USD, an estimate-based stage-admission limit.** No running
  211 stage is ever killed (author, 2026-11-10): before each stage the driver adds the pod time billed since allocation at the
  212 pod's actual hourly price, the charges of earlier failed attempts, and the stage's estimate, and stops between stages
  213 if the sum would pass the cap, then reports. Production runs refuse to start without `--started` (the allocation time)
  214 and `--usd-per-hour`; with `--prior-usd`, all are saved in `run.json`. A stage that overruns its estimate can end above
  215 the cap.
  216 
  217 **Launcher** (a copy of `results/pods/reset_pod.sh` with `podctl.py`, unchanged pod safety; draft
  218 `docs/revision/churn/churn_pod.sh`, installed in `results/pods/` after review): code and tarball guards; creation only
  219 before the hard deadline; the price checked against the ceiling `MAXCPH`; the memory floor (28 GiB); the gate, then the
  220 stages, each started only if no STOP file exists and the soft deadline has not passed (the soft deadline is the cap at
  221 the ceiling, net of earlier attempts' charges); at the soft deadline it writes STOP, so the pod stops after the running
  222 stage. **The one exception to "no running stage is killed":** the `podctl.py` watchdog deletes the pod at 1.3 times the
  223 cap at the price ceiling (10.40 USD at most for this arm), also mid-stage, as an absolute guard against a hung pod. Spend
  224 is recorded per attempt (pod id, start, end, recorded price) in the launcher's attempts file, which persists across
  225 launches; the summary gives the spend at the recorded prices and the bound at the ceiling. The launcher passes to the
  226 driver the current attempt's allocation time, the recorded price, and the earlier attempts' charges at their recorded
  227 prices. An EXIT, INT or TERM trap pulls the records, verifies them against the inventories, deletes the pod, and
  228 verifies the deletion; the watchdog keeps deleting until no pod of this name remains.
  229 
  230 ## 8. Steps
  231 
  232 1. Internal review of this plan to consensus; freeze it (commit).
  233 2. The author's approval of the follow-up limit is recorded; the reservation of 5.54 USD under `followup`.
  234 3. One pod: the p = 0 gate, then the 36 cells; delete the pod and verify the deletion; record the spend.
  235 4. Analysis in `docs/revision/posthoc/` (tested first); a paper paragraph and table, labelled exploratory.
